<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-statistics-measures-of-dispersion" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<title>Measures of Dispersion</title>

	<objectives>
			<ul>
			<li>
				<p>
					Calculate, describe, and interpret the measures of variation for a data set including the range, interquartile range (IQR), and standard deviation.
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<p>
					Find the quartiles of a data set.
				</p>
			</li>
		</ul>
	</objectives>

	<introduction>
		<p>
			A measure of center such as the mean is not sufficient to numerically summarize the characteristics of a data set. To see why this is the case, consider the following three sets of quiz scores for a 10-point quiz.
		</p>
		<p>
			<alert>Section A:</alert>
		</p>
		<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
			<row>
				<cell>5</cell>
				<cell>5</cell>
				<cell>5</cell>
				<cell>5</cell>
				<cell>5</cell>
				<cell>5</cell>
				<cell>5</cell>
				<cell>5</cell>
				<cell>5</cell>
				<cell>5</cell>
			</row>
		</tabular>
		<p>
			<alert>Section B:</alert>
		</p>
		<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
			<row>
				<cell>0</cell>
				<cell>0</cell>
				<cell>0</cell>
				<cell>0</cell>
				<cell>0</cell>
				<cell>10</cell>
				<cell>10</cell>
				<cell>10</cell>
				<cell>10</cell>
				<cell>10</cell>
			</row>
		</tabular>
		<p>
			<alert>Section C:</alert>
		</p>
		<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
			<row>
				<cell>4</cell>
				<cell>4</cell>
				<cell>4</cell>
				<cell>5</cell>
				<cell>5</cell>
				<cell>5</cell>
				<cell>5</cell>
				<cell>6</cell>
				<cell>6</cell>
				<cell>6</cell>
			</row>
		</tabular>
		<p>
			Notice that all three data sets have a mean of 5 points and median of 5 points, yet the sets of scores are clearly quite different from each other. In Section A, everyone had the same score; in Section B half the class earned no points and the other half got a perfect score. The scores for Section C vary a little, but they stay much closer to the central score of 5 than the scores in Section B do.
		</p>
		<p>
			Thus, in addition to the measures of center, we also need a measure of how <q>spread out</q> (or <q>varied</q>, or <q>dispersed</q>) each data set is in order to characterize it well.  There are several ways to measure the variation of a distribution. In this section we will look at the <alert>range</alert>, <alert>interquartile range (IQR)</alert>, and <alert>standard deviation</alert>.
		</p>
	</introduction>

	<subsection xml:id="subsec-dispersion-range">
		<title>Range</title>
		<p>
			The simplest way to quantify the spread of a data set is to compute the <term>range</term>. The range tells us the <q>width</q> of the entire data set by taking the difference between the maximum and minimum value.
		</p>
		<definition xml:id="def_range">
			<idx>range</idx>
			<idx><h>measures of spread</h><h>range</h></idx>
			<statement>
				<p>
					The <term>range</term> of a set of data is the difference between the highest and lowest values in the data set. That is:
				<me>
					\text{range} = \text{maximum} - \text{minimum}
				</me>.
			</p>
			</statement>
		</definition>
		<p>
			While simple to compute, the range is not a very useful measure of variation because it is heavily influenced by extreme values, or <xref ref="def_outlier" text="custom">outliers</xref>. For example, consider the distribution of incomes of full-time workers in the United States. While many people earn near minimum wage, a few individuals<mdash/>such as CEOs of major corporations<mdash/>earn tens of millions of dollars or more. This creates an enormous range that reveals little about where most incomes actually fall or how they are distributed around the typical (<q>center</q>) value.
		</p>
		<example xml:id="ex_center-spread_range_computation">
			<statement>
				<p>
					A certain pond has been well stocked with fish for the upcoming fishing season. A fish caught in this pond may be kept only if it is at least 13 inches long. To try to determine if the fish in the pond are long enough to allow fishing, a fish and game warden randomly catches and releases 20 different fish, finding the following lengths, in inches. Find the range of these data.
				</p>
				<table xml:id="ex_center-spread_range_computation_data">
					<title>Fish lengths in inches</title>
					<tabular>
						<row>
							<cell>13.2</cell>
							<cell>10.8</cell>
							<cell>14.6</cell>
							<cell>17.2</cell>
							<cell>17</cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell>18.3</cell>
							<cell>6.5</cell>
							<cell>13.4</cell>
							<cell>16.7</cell>
							<cell>11.3</cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell>12.3</cell>
							<cell>9.8</cell>
							<cell>9.4</cell>
							<cell>11.6</cell>
							<cell>12.1</cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell>8.7</cell>
							<cell>12.6</cell>
							<cell>13</cell>
							<cell>14.3</cell>
							<cell>13.1</cell>
						</row>
					</tabular>
				</table>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					To find the range we need to identify the maximum and minimum values in the data set. The largest value is 18.3 and the smallest is 6.5. Thus the range is:
					<me>
						\text{range} = 18.3 - 6.5 = 11.8
					</me>.
					That is, the range in observed fish lengths is 11.8 inches.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</example>
	</subsection>
	<subsection xml:id="subsec_spread_IQR-quartiles">
		<title>Interquartile Range (IQR)</title>
	<p>
		Recall from the previous section that the <xref ref="def_median" text="custom">median</xref> is resistant to outliers. To create a similarly resistant measure of variation, we can extend the logic used to create the median. The median divides data into two halves. If we further divide each half at its median, we create four quarters; the values that divide them are called <term>quartiles</term>.
		The <term>interquartile range (IQR)</term> measures the spread of the middle 50% of the data by finding the distance between the first quartile (<m>Q_1</m>) and third quartile (<m>Q_3</m>). Since the IQR ignores the lowest 25% and the highest 25% of the data, it is resistant to outliers.
	</p>
	<definition xml:id="def_quartiles">
			<idx>quartiles</idx>
			<idx><h>measures of relative standing</h><h>quartiles</h></idx>
			<statement>
				<p>Quantitative data can be divided up into quarters using five <term>quartiles</term>. These are:
				  <ul>
				  	<li>
							<p>
								the <term>zeroth quartile</term> (<m>Q_0</m>), which is the minimum value in the data set.
							</p>
						</li>
				  	<li>
							<p>
								the <term>first quartile</term> (<m>Q_1</m>), which divides the bottom quarter of the data from the rest of the data.
							</p>
						</li>
				  	<li>
							<p>
								the <term>second quartile</term> (<m>Q_2</m>), which divides the bottom half of the data from the top half. This is more commonly called the median.
							</p>
						</li>
				  	<li>
							<p>
								the <term>third quartile</term> (<m>Q_3</m>), which divides the bottom three-quarters of the data from the top quarter.
							</p>
						</li>
				  	<li>
							<p>
								the <term>fourth quartile</term> (<m>Q_4</m>), which is the maximum value in the data set.
							</p>
						</li>
				  </ul>
				</p>
			</statement>
		</definition>
		<definition xml:id="def_iqr">
			<idx>interquartile range (IQR)</idx>
			<idx><h>measures of spread</h><h>interquartile range (IQR)</h></idx>
			<statement>
				<p>
					The <term>interquartile range</term> or <term>IQR</term> is the difference between the third and first quartiles. That is,
				<me>
					\text{IQR} = Q_3 - Q_1
				</me>.
			</p>
			</statement>
		</definition>
		<p>
			Note that there are different methods for computing quartiles, so your results may vary when using spreadsheets or computer algebra systems. The following examples demonstrate how we will compute <m>Q_1</m> and <m>Q_3</m> in this book. If you obtain different values using technology, consult your instructor.
		</p>
		<example>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Suppose we have measured the height, in inches, of 12 women. The data values are listed below. Find the interquartile range.
				</p>
				<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
					<row>
						<cell>59</cell>
						<cell>60</cell>
						<cell>69</cell>
						<cell>64</cell>
						<cell>70</cell>
						<cell>72</cell>
						<cell>66</cell>
						<cell>64</cell>
						<cell>67</cell>
						<cell>66</cell>
						<cell>63</cell>
						<cell>61</cell>
					</row>
				</tabular>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					First, order the data.
				</p>
				<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
					<row>
						<cell>59</cell>
						<cell>60</cell>
						<cell>61</cell>
						<cell>63</cell>
						<cell>64</cell>
						<cell>64</cell>
						<cell>66</cell>
						<cell>66</cell>
						<cell>67</cell>
						<cell>69</cell>
						<cell>70</cell>
						<cell>72</cell>
					</row>
				</tabular>
				<p>
					Next, divide the data into two halves. In the case of an even sample size, we split the distribution down the middle. The first 6 data values are the lower half and the next 6 data values are the upper half. Then we find the median of each half. The median of the lower half is <m>Q_1</m> and the median of the upper half is <m>Q_3</m>.
				</p>
				<p>
					<md>
					<mrow>\overbrace{59,60,\underbrace{61,63,}_{Q_1=\frac{61+63}{2}=62} 64,64,}^{\text{Lower Half}} \overbrace{66,66,\underbrace{67, 69,}_{Q_3 =\frac{67+69}{2}=68} 70,72}^{\text{Upper Half}}</mrow>
				</md>
				</p>
				<p>
					In this data <m>Q_{1}=62</m> inches, and <m>Q_{3}=68</m> inches. The IQR is thus
					<me>
						\text{IQR} = Q_{3}-Q_{1} = 68 - 62 = 6\text{ inches}
					</me>.
					This tells us that the middle 50% of the women's heights lie within an interval 6 inches wide that contains the median height of 65 inches. Said another way, 50% of the heights fall between 62 and 68 inches.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</example>
		<example>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Suppose we added one more height (68 inches) to the data set from the previous example. Find the interquartile range of the heights.
				</p>
				<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
					<row>
						<cell>59</cell>
						<cell>60</cell>
						<cell>61</cell>
						<cell>63</cell>
						<cell>64</cell>
						<cell>64</cell>
						<cell>66</cell>
						<cell>66</cell>
						<cell>67</cell>
						<cell>68</cell>
						<cell>69</cell>
						<cell>70</cell>
						<cell>72</cell>
					</row>
				</tabular>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					The data are already in order, but now we have an odd number of values.  We use the median value of 66 as both the maximum value for the lower half of the data and the minimum value for the upper half.
					<me>
						\overbrace{59,60, 61, \underbrace{63,}_{Q_1=63} 64, 64, 6}^{\text{Lower Half}} \overbrace{6, 66, 67, \underbrace{68,}_{Q_3=68} 69, 70, 72}^{\text{Upper Half}}
					</me>
					The interquartile range is then
					<me>
						\text{IQR} = Q_{3} - Q_{1} = 68 - 63 = 5\text{ inches}	
					</me>.
					That is, the middle 50% of the women's heights lie within an interval 5 inches wide that contains the median height of 66 inches. In other words, 50% of the heights are between 63 and 68 inches.
				</p>
				<p>
					Including the median in both halves when <m>n</m> is odd is known as Tukey's method. Calculators such as the TI-84 set the median aside instead, which here gives <m>Q_1 = 62</m> and <m>Q_3 = 68.5</m>, so technology may give slightly different quartiles.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</example>
	</subsection>
	<subsection xml:id="subsec-dispersion-standard_deviation">
	<title>Standard Deviation</title>
		<p>
			While the interquartile range is a good measure of spread, sometimes there are no outliers in a data set, or a measure of spread that takes all values into account is desirable. In this case, the <term>standard deviation</term> is used to describe variation. The <term>sample standard deviation</term>, denoted <m>s</m>, is a measure of variation that tells us roughly how far the data values typically are from the mean.
		</p>
		<definition xml:id="def_std_dev">
			<idx>standard deviation</idx>
			<idx><h>measures of spread</h><h>standard deviation</h></idx>
			<statement>
				<p> The <term>sample standard deviation</term>, <m>s</m>, measures the typical distance of the data values from the mean of a data set. It is given by
				<me>
					s=\sqrt{\frac{\text{sum of the squared deviations from the mean}}{n-1}}
				</me>,
				where <m>n</m> is the sample size.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</definition>
		<p>
			The following example walks through the whole process of computing a standard deviation.
		</p>
		<example>
			<p>
				Returning to the quiz example used in the introduction of this section as motivation, let's suppose there is a fourth set of quiz scores for a 10-point quiz. The mean quiz score for Section D, like Sections A, B and C, is 5 points.
			</p>
			<p>
				<alert>Section D:</alert>
			</p>
			<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
				<row>
					<cell>0</cell>
					<cell>5</cell>
					<cell>5</cell>
					<cell>5</cell>
					<cell>5</cell>
					<cell>5</cell>
					<cell>5</cell>
					<cell>5</cell>
					<cell>5</cell>
					<cell>10</cell>
				</row>
			</tabular>
			<p>
				The first step in finding the standard deviation is to find the deviation, or difference, of each data value from the mean. We will do this in a table. You could also use a spreadsheet to do these calculations.
			</p>
			<tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
				<row header="yes">
					<cell><alert>Data Value</alert></cell>
					<cell><alert>Deviation: (Data Value <m>-</m> Mean)</alert></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell>0</cell>
					<cell><m>0-5=-5</m></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell>5</cell>
					<cell><m>5-5=0</m></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell>5</cell>
					<cell><m>5-5=0</m></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell>5</cell>
					<cell><m>5-5=0</m></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell>5</cell>
					<cell><m>5-5=0</m></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell>5</cell>
					<cell><m>5-5=0</m></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell>5</cell>
					<cell><m>5-5=0</m></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell>5</cell>
					<cell><m>5-5=0</m></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell>5</cell>
					<cell><m>5-5=0</m></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell>10</cell>
					<cell><m>10-5=5</m></cell>
				</row>
			</tabular>
			<p>
				We want to find the <q>average</q> deviation from the mean, but if we find the average of the values in the second column, the negative and positive values cancel each other out (this will always happen), so to prevent this we square the deviations so that none of them are negative.
			</p>
			<tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
				<row header="yes">
					<cell><alert>Data Value</alert></cell>
					<cell><alert>Deviation: (Data Value <m>-</m> Mean)</alert></cell>
					<cell><alert>Deviation Squared</alert></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell>0</cell>
					<cell><m>0-5=-5</m></cell>
					<cell><m>(-5)^{2}=25</m></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell>5</cell>
					<cell><m>5-5=0</m></cell>
					<cell><m>0^{2}=0</m></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell>5</cell>
					<cell><m>5-5=0</m></cell>
					<cell><m>0^{2}=0</m></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell>5</cell>
					<cell><m>5-5=0</m></cell>
					<cell><m>0^{2}=0</m></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell>5</cell>
					<cell><m>5-5=0</m></cell>
					<cell><m>0^{2}=0</m></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell>5</cell>
					<cell><m>5-5=0</m></cell>
					<cell><m>0^{2}=0</m></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell>5</cell>
					<cell><m>5-5=0</m></cell>
					<cell><m>0^{2}=0</m></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell>5</cell>
					<cell><m>5-5=0</m></cell>
					<cell><m>0^{2}=0</m></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell>5</cell>
					<cell><m>5-5=0</m></cell>
					<cell><m>0^{2}=0</m></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell>10</cell>
					<cell><m>10-5=5</m></cell>
					<cell><m>5^{2}=25</m></cell>
				</row>
			</tabular>
			<p>
				Next, we add the squared deviations and we get
				<me>
					25+0+0+0+0+0+0+0+0+25=50
				</me>.
			</p>
			<p>
				To find the mean of the squared deviations, we would typically divide this total (50) by the number of scores, <m>n</m> (in this case, 10). However, we divide by <m>n</m> only when the data represents a population. For sample data (which is more common), we divide by <m>n-1</m> (in this case, 9).
			</p>
			<p>
				We assume Section D represents a sample, so we will divide by 9. The result, <m>50/9 \approx 5.56</m>, is called the <term>sample variance</term> and is written <m>s^2</m>. Note that its units are <q>points-squared</q> because we squared all of the deviations. It is more meaningful to use the units we started with, so we take the square root of this quantity to convert back to <q>points</q>.
			</p>
			<p>
				Thus, the sample standard deviation for Section D is
				<me>
					s=\sqrt{\frac{50}{9}} \approx 2.36\text{ points}
				</me>.
				This means that, roughly speaking, a typical score in Section D is about 2.36 points away from the mean of 5 points. For comparison, the standard deviations for the other sections are:
				<ul>
					<li>
						<p><m>s_{A}=0</m> points,</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p><m>s_{B}=5.27</m> points,</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p><m>s_{C}=0.82</m> points,</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p><m>s_{D}=2.36</m> points.</p>
					</li>
				</ul>
				Apart from Section A, where every score is the same, Section C has the smallest standard deviation, so its scores are the least spread out. Section B has the largest, since every score in it is as far from the mean as a score on this quiz can be. These values match what we observe when we look at the data.
			</p>
		</example>
		<p>
			Here are a few important characteristics to keep in mind when finding and interpreting the standard deviation.
			<ul>
				<li>
					<p>
						The standard deviation is never negative. It will be zero if all the data values are equal and get larger as the data <q>spreads out</q>.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						The standard deviation has the same units as the original data, and it is important to label it.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						The standard deviation, like the mean, can be influenced by outliers.
					</p>
				</li>
			</ul>
		</p>
		<assemblage>
			<title>Calculating the Sample Standard Deviation</title>
			<p>
				<ol>
					<li>
						<p>
							Find the mean of the data.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							Find the deviations by subtracting the mean from each data value.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							Square each deviation.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							Sum the squared deviations.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							Divide the sum of the squared deviations by <m>n-1</m> and take the square root.
						</p>
					</li>
				</ol>
			</p>
		</assemblage>	
		<example>
			<statement>
				<p>
					The price of a pint of peanut butter at 5 stores was $3.29, $3.59, $3.79, $3.75, and $3.99. Find the sample standard deviation of these data.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					First we need to calculate the sample mean:
					<me>
						\bar{x}=\frac{3.29 + 3.59 + 3.79 + 3.75+ 3.99}{5} = \$3.68
					</me>.
					Keep in mind we divide by <m>n=5</m> here.
				</p>
				<p>
					Next, we need to find the deviation from the mean for each data value and square it. This is easiest done in a table.
				</p>
				<tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
					<row header="yes">
						<cell><alert>Data Value</alert></cell>
						<cell><alert>Deviation</alert></cell>
						<cell><alert>Deviation Squared</alert></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>$3.29</cell>
						<cell><m>3.29-3.68=-0.39</m></cell>
						<cell><m>(-0.39)^{2}=0.1521</m></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>$3.59</cell>
						<cell><m>3.59-3.68=-0.09</m></cell>
						<cell><m>(-0.09)^{2}=0.0081</m></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>$3.79</cell>
						<cell><m>3.79-3.68=0.11</m></cell>
						<cell><m>(0.11)^{2}=0.0121</m></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>$3.75</cell>
						<cell><m>3.75-3.68=0.07</m></cell>
						<cell><m>(0.07)^{2}=0.0049</m></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>$3.99</cell>
						<cell><m>3.99-3.68=0.31</m></cell>
						<cell><m>(0.31)^{2}=0.0961</m></cell>
					</row>
				</tabular>
				<p>
					The sum of the deviations squared is
					<me>		
						0.1521+0.0081+0.0121+0.0049+0.0961=0.2733
					</me>.
					Therefore, the sample standard deviation is
					<me>
						s=\sqrt{\frac{0.2733}{4}} \approx \$0.2614
					</me>.
					Since the units are dollars, we round to two decimal places. This gives us a standard deviation of $0.26. This means that, roughly speaking, the price of a typical pint of peanut butter in our data set is about $0.26 away from the mean price of $3.68.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</example>
	</subsection>
</section>